addclaim(75,918,5,[66,67,68,69],'1–83','Paraphrase: limits of models and evaluations are computed pointwise, with the dual assertion for colimits.','For a doctrine prescribing specified limits and colimits, a J-limit of models is pointwise when it exists in the target and commutes there with the prescribed colimits; limits commute with the prescribed limits. Change of target preserves this construction when the target functor preserves the J-limit. The dual statement exchanges limits and colimits.','proof sketched here; omitted preservation hypothesis supplied by himself in file07:415','For each prescribed cone, interchange the two limits. For a prescribed cocone use the stated interchange isomorphism. The cone universality is inherited from the functor category, since the model subcategory is full. Evaluation and precomposition then preserve these pointwise limits. A target functor with no preservation condition cannot be used in that last step.','Lack–Tendas Proposition5.12 and Corollaries5.13–5.14 give the model-category limit framework; the elementary interchange proof here makes the exact target hypotheses explicit. The later manuscript formulation is the corrected one.',['LackTendas2023'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':394–425');claims[-1]['scans'] += [104]
addclaim(76,918,5,[67,68],'42–83','Paraphrase: models in functor categories, and fully faithful target change, are controlled on basic objects.','T(Fun(J,C))≃Fun(J,T(C)) for pointwise doctrine operations. A faithful, respectively fully faithful, operation-preserving C→D induces the same property on models. If a specified basis generates the representing category by the prescribed operations, the model square over that basis is a 2-pullback for a fully faithful C→D.','proof sketched; expanded in file07 scans105–106','Currying identifies the first two categories and all natural transformations. For the last assertion lift the basis objects and their structure arrows through the fully faithful embedding, then extend along the specified operations; their universal properties give unique coherent lifts. Mere determination of transformations on a faithful basis does not establish the generation hypothesis; C101 separates them.','Kelly5.35 supplies the free-completion universal property; Lack–Tendas5.12–5.14 covers the bounded sketch setting. The lift argument is independently checked and is explicitly conditional on genuine operation generation.',['Kelly1982','LackTendas2023'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':423–554');claims[-1]['scans'] += [105,106]
addclaim(77,918,5,[68,69],'45–83','Paraphrase: models in C are Set-model-valued presheaves with representable underlying constructions.','For a small limits-only theory R, S=Hom_lambda(R,Set), and a locally small lambda-complete C, models R→C identify with functors C^op→S whose construction presheaves are representable. A genuinely operation-generating basis suffices. The evaluation functor R→Fun(S,Set) is fully faithful.','sketch; one paragraph canceled, later proof in file07 scans106–107','Yoneda C→PSh(C) preserves limits; currying gives Hom_lambda(R,PSh(C))≃Fun(C^op,S). A model lands in the fully faithful representable image exactly when its component presheaves are representable. Closure of representables under the designated limits reduces this to a generating basis. Evaluation at r is represented in S by Hom_R(r,-), which preserves all existing limits; two applications of Yoneda give Nat(ev_r,ev_s)≃Hom_R(r,s).','Kelly5.35 and ordinary Yoneda identify the exact completion mechanism. This statement retains the selected constructions; it is not an intrinsic recovery from S alone. The canceled early paragraph is not treated as a second independent theorem.',['Kelly1982','RiehlContextOnline'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':490–575');claims[-1]['scans'] += [106,107]
addclaim(78,919,5,[70],'85–103','Paraphrase: no operations gives small categories; groupoids and ordered categories are quotient doctrines.','A no-operation small presentation represents a small category. Inverting all arrows gives its groupoid completion; identifying parallel arrows gives its reachability preorder; further identifying mutually comparable objects gives its poset reflection.','stated as known examples','Formal words of arrows and inverses, modulo compositions and inverse relations, have the groupoid universal property. The preorder has one arrow exactly when a path exists. Its antisymmetrization identifies x and y when paths exist both ways. A preorder need not already be a poset, so those last two constructions must remain distinct.','Stacks04VB gives the universal localization framework; the preorder and poset quotients are checked directly. This is an elementary settled construction, not a new priority assertion about the undated sheet.',['StacksLocalization'])
addclaim(79,919,5,[71],'104–115','Paraphrase: the envelope for all small projective limits is the opposite of a presheaf category.','For small I, its free completion under all small limits is Fun(I,Set)^op, in an enlarged universe. Continuous functors out of it correspond to arbitrary functors I→C into a complete target.','proved there by a displayed chain of equivalences','Dualize the free cocompletion PSh(I^op)=Fun(I,Set): an object is a small colimit of representables, so the dual extension is the corresponding limit in C. Natural transformations extend uniquely by these universal properties. The completion is generally not small in the original universe.','Kelly Theorem5.35 and the all-indexing-types example immediately following it, pp99–100, give the exact free cocompletion theorem. The target-variable ambiguity in the displayed manuscript formula is separately registered.',['Kelly1982'])
addclaim(80,919,5,[71,72],'116–149','Paraphrase: a suitably saturated calculus of fractions solves all-limit localization; strict cogenerators pass through successive stages.','A right-fraction localization preserves existing finite limits. If the denominator class is additionally closed under the relevant products, the same roof argument preserves those products. Local smallness of the saturated localization and preservation of the manuscript’s strict-cogenerator condition require separate hypotheses; the proposed transitivity is explicitly withdrawn.','partial proof; size doubts and a canceled transitivity step','Pullbacks put finitely many roofs over a common denominator and prove finite-limit universality. For an arbitrary product, the product of the component denominators is a denominator by hypothesis; this gives the required common roof. These arguments do not bound the set of all roof classes for a large category. Nor does a Cat colimit by itself establish strict cogeneration in a later doctrine completion.','Stacks04VB Lemmas4.27.9 and4.27.17 prove the exact finite-limit fraction result, following Gabriel–ZismanI.3.1. Kelly5.35 gives a bounded small completion, not the unbounded strict-cogenerator theorem. The remaining local-smallness and generation issue is therefore retained as a precise gap.',['StacksLocalization','Kelly1982'],'C')
addclaim(81,919,5,[72,73,74],'132–184','Paraphrase: continuous Set-valued functors are representable and continuous functors have left adjoints when there is a small cogenerating family.','The asserted conclusions hold for a complete locally small source with a small coseparating family and intersections of every collection of subobjects of a fixed object. Well-poweredness plus completeness is one sufficient way to obtain the intersection condition. A bare small cogenerator is not the full stated theorem.','theorem asserted with changes to the word strict and an incomplete sufficiency argument','The special adjoint functor theorem constructs initial objects in each comma category by intersecting subobjects of a product of cogenerators. For F:R→Set continuous, its left adjoint L then gives F(X)≃Hom_R(L1,X), establishing representability. With this package, the all-limit reconstruction and its contravariant adjoint dictionary follow. Without it, the changing manuscript cogenerator terminology does not supply the missing solution-set argument.','Riehl Theorem4.7.10 and Lemma4.7.11 pp178–179 were read with their proofs; their exact intersection hypothesis is retained. The manuscript’s broader wording is not identified with this theorem by terminology alone.',['RiehlContextOnline'],'C')
addclaim(82,919,5,[75,76,77],'186–253','Paraphrase: close under bounded shapes, embed into the opposite of a model category, then localize.','For a small category and a small set of small limit shapes, the free completion is small and obtained by a regular-bounded transfinite closure. Yoneda gives the fully faithful embedding into Hom_lambda(C,Set)^op when C has the designated limits. Any further localization construction must retain local-smallness and preservation hypotheses.','proof sketched; embedding question answered at213; final smallness query canceled at253','Dualize Kelly5.35. At a regular ordinal above the sizes of all specified shapes, every diagram in the union has appeared before that stage, so closure stabilizes. The evaluation embedding is fully faithful because covariant representables are models and detect natural transformations. One-step closure works for the family of all finite products but is not valid for arbitrary shape families; Kelly gives explicit multi-stage examples.','Kelly5.35pp99–100 supplies the exact smallness and free-completion result, including the warning that one closure step need not suffice. This answers the bounded-shape part and isolates the large localization issue already recorded in C080.',['Kelly1982'])
addclaim(83,920,5,[78,79],'255–306','Paraphrase: finitely complete R is recovered oppositely from the finitely presentable objects of S=Lex(R,Set).','For small finitely complete R, Lex(R,Set)≃Ind(R^op), and its finitely presentable subcategory is equivalent to R^op. This extends to Gabriel–Ulmer duality with filtered-colimit-preserving right adjoints on the model side.','proof sketched with two notational variance alarms','Every lex functor is a filtered colimit of covariant representables. A finitely presentable object is a retract of a representable; R is idempotent complete because it has equalizers, so that retract is representable. This proves the recovery and identifies the variance. It uses finite colimits on the dual small subcategory, not arbitrary small colimits.','Lack–Power2009 Theorem8p270 states the exact biequivalence. Kelly5.10 supplies the filtered-colimit/left-exact description; Makkai–Pitts1987 provides related intrinsic criteria. The original Gabriel–Ulmer monograph is a backward reference, not a claimed first-edition proof inspection.',['LackPower2009','Kelly1982','MakkaiPitts1987'])
addclaim(84,920,5,[79],'332–336','Paraphrase: the model functor should preserve the finitely presentable objects.','For locally finitely presentable S,S′ and L⊣U:S′→S, U preserves filtered colimits exactly when L sends finitely presentable objects of S to finitely presentable objects of S′. The manuscript first puts the latter condition on U and later moves it to its left adjoint.','corrected by himself later, file06 scan91 line379','For a finitely presentable a, Hom(L a,-)≃Hom(a,U-); commutation with filtered colimits gives the forward implication. Conversely the finitely presentable a form a strong generator, so these Hom comparisons detect that U commutes with filtered colimits. U:Groups→Set does preserve filtered colimits but sends the finitely presentable group Z to an infinite, non-finitely-presentable set, refuting the first wording.','Lack–Power Theorem8 uses precisely filtered-colimit-preserving right adjoints. The adjoint equivalence above is independently proved and locates the manuscript’s own correction exactly.',['LackPower2009'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(6)+':379');claims[-1]['scans'] += [91]
addclaim(85,920,5,[79],'339–340','Paraphrase: can one recover a finite-product theory from its Set-model category, and find all its presentations?','A category of models determines the finite-product presentation only up to the appropriate Morita/Cauchy completion; the selected finite-coproduct generators remain presentation data. Strongly finitely presentable projectives, not all finitely presentable objects, are relevant for algebraic theories.','open on the pages','FinSet and its full subcategory of even finite sets both have finite coproducts and the same idempotent completion. Every finite set is a retract of an even finite set. Their opposite finite-product theories have equivalent Set-model categories because idempotents split in Set, but the presentation categories are not equivalent.','Adámek–Lawvere–Rosický2003p36 gives this exact example; Lemma2.1, Corollary2.4, Proposition2.6 and Theorem4.1 give the projective and Cauchy-completion recovery. This settles the literal uniqueness question and supplies the precise classification framework.',['AdamekLawvereRosicky2003'])

addproblem(40,'Pointwise model limits and change of target',[occurrence(5,[66,67,68,69],'1–83','early proof with omitted hypothesis'),occurrence(7,[104],'394–425','preservation hypothesis inserted at415')],'Do limits of models and evaluations commute with target change?','Yes under the interchange and target-preservation conditions in C075; the dual colimit statement is analogous.','C075–077 and Lack–Tendas5.12–5.14 settle the bounded model version.',['LackTendas2023','Kelly1982'],worth='Commentary work only: record the later preservation hypothesis and make the pointwise interchange proof explicit.')
addproblem(41,'Size and cogeneration in all-limit localization',[occurrence(5,[71,72],'116–149','local-smallness doubts; strict transitivity withdrawn')],'Does saturation preserve a U-category, and do strict cogenerators survive the successive envelope constructions?','Finite limits and products are preserved under the stated fraction and product-stability conditions; local smallness and strict cogeneration require independent size hypotheses.','C080 matches Stacks4.27.17 and identifies the remaining large-category issue; Kelly5.35 settles bounded completions only.',['StacksLocalization','Kelly1982'],worth='Worth a bounded reconstruction task: fix the exact strict-cogenerator definition and test the first localization where small Hom sets are not automatic.')
addproblem(42,'The all-limit representation theorem',[occurrence(5,[72,73,74],'132–184')],'Which small cogenerator condition represents every continuous Set-valued functor and gives all required adjoints?','Use the full special adjoint functor theorem package, including arbitrary subobject intersections.','C081 and Riehl4.7.10–4.7.11 give the exact proof and explain which premise the manuscript does not verify.',['RiehlContextOnline'],worth='Worth a precise historical commentary, with a limited mathematical task of translating the manuscript’s varying strictness condition into the solution-set hypothesis.')
addproblem(43,'Bounded free envelopes and the canceled smallness query',[occurrence(5,[75,76,77],'186–253','embedding answered at213; final query canceled'),occurrence(7,[103],'317–350')],'Can one freely add the specified operations and retain a small classifier?','Yes for a small set of small limit shapes and a small starting category; mixed exactness and large presentations require further conditions.','C082 and Kelly5.35 prove the bounded assertion, including the regular-cofinality step.',['Kelly1982'],worth='Commentary work for the limits-only theorem; further work is worthwhile only after choosing one explicit mixed doctrine and its size bound.')
addproblem(44,'Finite-limit versus all-limit theories',[occurrence(5,[79],'326–330','1-faithful explicitly defined'),occurrence(7,[103],'351–358','equivalence versus full faithfulness queried')],'Does restriction to complete targets identify all morphisms and all constructions?','It can be locally fully faithful in the stated finite-limit comparison without being essentially surjective on Hom categories; complete targets admit new infinite-power constructions.','C102 below gives the counterexample and the completion framework from Kelly5.35.',['Kelly1982'],worth='Worth a short commentary clarification: separate full faithfulness of the comparison functor from equivalence of its Hom categories.')
addproblem(45,'Which adjoint preserves finite presentation?',[occurrence(5,[79],'332–336','first formulation'),occurrence(6,[91],'379','corrected to the left adjoint')],'What is the essential-image condition on the model functor?','The right adjoint preserves filtered colimits iff its left adjoint preserves finitely presentable objects.','C084 gives the Hom proof and Groups→Set counterexample; Lack–Power8 matches the repaired dictionary.',['LackPower2009'],worth='Commentary work only: the pages themselves repair the statement, and the adjunction proof is complete.')
addproblem(46,'Recovering finite-product theories',[occurrence(5,[79],'339–340')],'Is a finite-product theory determined by its Set models, and how are its possible presentations found?','Recover its Morita class and the appropriate projective Cauchy completion; preserve chosen generators as additional data.','C085 matches the exact even-finite-set example and ALR2003 Proposition2.6/Theorem4.1.',['AdamekLawvereRosicky2003'],worth='A bounded comparison is worthwhile: identify the chosen generators in one manuscript example and compare its Cauchy completion, rather than seek uniqueness already known to fail.')

